{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"decrepit-blowy-javalina","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Report\n\nOperator observed hundreds of open Jira issues, including duplicates, and suspected the reconciler bridge was mass-creating duplicates. Prime suspect was change 2657 (ticket `5c5c`, \"Reconcile Bridge ACLI 1200-ceiling outage\"), which replaced a single ACLI query with half-open `created` windows.\n\n## Verdict\n\n**The reconciler behaved to spec. Change 2657 is REFUTED.** Two independent, unrelated upstream events explain every symptom.\n\n### Event A — the Jira volume surge (09-04/05)\n\nA bulk LOCAL ticket generator created ~1,700 rebar tickets in ~5 hours, one task per repo file, titled `Condense comments in <path>`. CREATE-event commits per UTC hour on the `tickets` branch:\n\n    19:00  7 | 20:00  62 | 21:00 184 | 22:00 968 | 23:00 318 | 00:00 6 | 01:00 147\n\nAll are `ticket_type=task`, syncable, unbound — so every one is a legitimate outbound CREATE. The reconciler mirrored them faithfully.\n\nPer-pass `RECON: outbound_differ` from GitHub Actions (workflow `reconcile-bridge.yml`):\n\n    09-04 15:28..20:27  create=0..4     <- normal steady state\n    09-04 21:25         create=138      <- ONSET\n    09-04 22:25         create=520\n    09-04 23:24         create=1180\n    09-05 00:59         create=985\n    09-05 01:33,02:34,03:31  FAILED (ACLI 1200-issue ceiling)\n    09-05 05:27         create=942\n    09-05 06:38         create=746\n    09-05 07:27         create=547\n    09-05 08:31         create=355\n    09-05 09:26         create=156\n    09-05 10:25..14:25  create=0..5     <- drained, back to normal\n\nThe ACLI 1,200-ceiling outage was a **consequence**, not a cause: the growing not-Done working set crossed the per-query ceiling. Change 2657 landed 2026-09-05T04:35:48Z — **after** both the onset (21:25) and the outage it fixed.\n\n### Event B — the duplicates (08-31/09-01)\n\n425 excess rebar tickets across 89 normalized titles, created 2026-08-31 (233) and 2026-09-01 (172), by repeated janitor/comment-triage sweeps re-filing identical findings (`comment triage: llm core` x9, `a2-1: move last_get_pass rotation bookkeeping...` x12). These reached Jira as REB-665..REB-4415 — days **before** the 09-04 surge. **Zero** duplicate-title tickets were created during the incident window.\n\n## Decisive experiment — the binding store exonerates the reconciler\n\n`.bridge_state/bindings.json` on the `tickets` branch is git-versioned. Entry count per pass:\n\n    09-04 20:35  3626\n    09-04 21:48  3764   (+138 == create=138)\n    09-04 22:53  3964   (+200)\n    09-04 23:51  4164   (+200)\n    09-05 01:23  4364   (+200)\n    09-05 05:55  4564   (+200)\n    09-05 07:02  4764   (+200)\n    09-05 07:58  4964   (+200)\n    09-05 09:02  5164   (+200)\n    09-05 09:46  5320   (+156 == create=156)\n    09-05 10:37  5325   (+5)\n    09-05 11:32  5327   (+2)\n    09-05 12:40  5332   (+5)\n    09-05 13:32  5332   (+0)\n    09-05 14:33  5333   (+1)\n\nMonotonic, never truncated, and each increment matches the pass's create count exactly whenever it is below 200. Every proposed create produced exactly one persisted binding.\n\nLive binding store (5,333 entries): **5,333 distinct Jira keys; zero Jira keys bound to more than one local ticket.** The reconciler created exactly one Jira issue per rebar ticket.\n\n## Mechanism review (why duplication was impossible here)\n\n- Create-vs-update keys off the **persisted binding store**, not the snapshot: `outbound_differ.py:578` `jira_key = binding_store.get_jira_key(local_id)`, then `:588 if jira_key is None:` -> create, else update.\n- A bound ticket absent from the snapshot goes to the UPDATE branch and defers; only a live HTTP 200 GET yields an update (`outbound_mutation_builders.py:255-292`). This is bug `1e08-1a35-0267-4ca6`'s fix — discriminate on MEMBERSHIP, not value.\n- Snapshot JQL (`fetcher.py:89-97`) is not-Done UNION the 1,000 most-recently-updated Done issues. Snapshot incompleteness therefore cannot route a bound ticket to create.\n- None of `9e65567b79ff`, `e8fbf305057b`, `084e753d30df`, `5ae93de838af`, `a33333b8709a` changed the JQL, the statusCategory filter, the field list, or outbound scope.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] Confirm with the operator that the ~1,700 `Condense comments in <path>` tickets were intended, or retract them.\n- [ ] Decide the disposition of the 425 duplicate rebar tickets (389 still not closed) and their Jira mirrors REB-665..REB-4415.\n- [ ] Re-enable the `Reconcile Bridge` workflow once (a) and (b) are decided.\n\n## Containment already taken\n\n`gh workflow disable reconcile-bridge.yml --repo navapbc/rebar` at ~2026-09-05T15:00Z, operator-authorised. Last pass was run `33971771591`, started 14:25Z, 8.4 min, success, create=1. `Reconciler Heartbeat Canary` will go RED by design while the bridge is disabled — that red is an artefact of containment, not a symptom.\n\n## Not a reconciler defect\n\nNo code change to the reconciler is warranted. The defects are upstream (duplicate ticket generation) and in observability (no aggregation/alarm on duplicate creation — tracked separately as `dbe7-d957-e5da-49dd`).","id":"bafc-2f85-836f-4bdd","parent_id":"","priority":1,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Jira open-issue surge 2026-09-04/05: bulk local ticket generation, not reconciler duplication"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1788621473294671564,"uuid":"5de7aa94-3f16-4103-8ac8-483b8972ede5"}